    {
      "source_number": "1075",
      "original_required_task": "Find the number of books, at most400, that leaves exactly1 book when packed in groups of4,5 or6 and leaves no book when packed in groups of7.",
      "actual_explicit_subparts": 1,
      "source_mathematics_status": "solved_unique_under_all_stated_conditions",
      "domain": "N is an integer book count with1<=N<=400. Remainder statements are ordinary nonnegative integer division. All four packing conditions are simultaneous.",
      "independent_solution": "The first three conditions say that4,5,6 all divide N-1. Their least common multiple is60, so N=60k+1. The bound gives integer k=0,...,6. The7-book condition gives60k+1=0 modulo7, equivalently4k+1=0 modulo7. Since the inverse of4 modulo7 is2, k=5 modulo7. Of0,...,6 only k=5 is possible. Thus N=301. Direct checks are301=4*75+1=5*60+1=6*50+1=7*43, and301<=400.",
      "exact_derivation_steps": [
        "lcm(4,5,6)=2^2*3*5=60.",
        "N-1 is a multiple of60, hence N=60k+1.",
        "1<=N<=400 implies0<=k<=399/60, so k is one of0,1,2,3,4,5,6.",
        "60=4 modulo7, so4k+1=0 modulo7.",
        "4k=6 modulo7 and2*4=1 modulo7 give k=12=5 modulo7.",
        "k=5 yields N=301 and satisfies each packing requirement."
      ],
      "complete_solution_set": [301],
      "boundary_and_uniqueness_qualifications": [
        "The upper limit400 is inclusive and eliminates all other members of the progression301+420t.",
        "The count0 cannot satisfy a remainder1 condition, so whether0 belongs to the natural numbers is irrelevant.",
        "The argument does not require an unstated minimum number of full bundles; the sole solution already gives positive bundles in all four packing schemes.",
        "Solving all simultaneous conditions, rather than any one packing scheme, is the original task."
      ],
      "unresolved_material_ambiguity": null
    },
    {
      "source_number": "1076",
      "original_required_task": "Prove that starting with one sheet, cutting it into4 pieces and repeatedly cutting some or all current pieces individually into4 cannot produce exactly50 pieces.",
      "actual_explicit_subparts": 1,
      "source_mathematics_status": "solved_impossibility_for_every_allowed_finite_cut_sequence",
      "domain": "All pieces produced are counted. One allowed elementary action replaces one existing piece by exactly4 pieces. A stage may choose any positive number of current pieces; simultaneous cuts are the sum of those elementary actions.",
      "independent_solution": "The initial piece count is1. Each elementary cut removes one existing piece and adds four, increasing the total by3. After m elementary cuts the total is therefore1+3m, with m a nonnegative integer and m>=1 after the mandated first cut. If a stage cuts r selected pieces, its increment is3r, so the same invariant covers 'some or all' and arbitrary later choices. Every possible count is1 modulo3, whereas50=3*16+2 is2 modulo3. Hence exactly50 pieces is impossible.",
      "exact_derivation_steps": [
        "Initial count P0=1.",
        "One replacement changes P to P-1+4=P+3.",
        "Replacing r selected current pieces changes P to P+3r.",
        "By induction, after any finite allowed sequence P=1+3m.",
        "50-1=49 is not divisible by3; there is no integer m with1+3m=50."
