      "actual_explicit_subparts": 3,
      "source_mathematics_status": "solved_all_three_explicit_parts",
      "domain": "Ordinary positive decimal four-digit numbers1000 through9999. The first digit is nonzero. Each position may repeat an allowed digit because no distinct-digit or multiplicity restriction is stated. Parity refers to the individual digits, not just the parity of the whole number.",
      "independent_solution": "For(a)(1), the odd digit alphabet is{1,3,5,7,9}. All five digits can occur in the first position, and all four positions each have five choices, giving5^4=625. For(a)(2), the even digit alphabet is{0,2,4,6,8}. The first position has only four choices2,4,6,8, while the remaining three positions each have five choices, giving4*5^3=500. For(b), there are9*10^3=9000 four-digit numbers in total. A number without both parities is either all-odd or all-even, and those two classes are disjoint. Thus the mixed-parity count is9000-625-500=7875.",
      "numbered_subpart_solutions": [
