      "domain": "Let X=a+b*sqrt(2) and Y=c+d*sqrt(2), where a,b,c,d are rational. Addition, subtraction and multiplication are defined for all such pairs. Quotient X/Y is defined only when Y!=0. The source prose does not explicitly state this division restriction.",
      "independent_solution": "For two arbitrary elements X=a+b*sqrt(2) and Y=c+d*sqrt(2), their sum is(a+c)+(b+d)*sqrt(2), their difference is(a-c)+(b-d)*sqrt(2), and their product is(ac+2bd)+(ad+bc)*sqrt(2). All coefficients are rational. For Y!=0, rationalize with its conjugate c-d*sqrt(2). The denominator D=c^2-2d^2 is nonzero for rational c,d unless c=d=0. To prove that claim, if d=0 then D=0 gives c=0. If d!=0 and D=0, the rational t=c/d would satisfy t^2=2. Write t=p/q in lowest terms, q>0. Then p^2=2q^2 forces p even, p=2r; hence q^2=2r^2 forces q even, contradicting coprimality. Thus no rational t has square2. Because Y!=0 excludes c=d=0, D!=0. Therefore X/Y=(ac-2bd)/D+((bc-ad)/D)*sqrt(2), again with rational coefficients. This proves quotient closure on its defined domain, not division by zero.",
      "operation_derivations": [
